### The Bidirectional Passthrough Bridge Sketch

This sketch establishes a transparent bridge between the PC's USB Serial Monitor and an external device on virtual pins 10 and 11.

```cpp
// Monster Lab - SoftwareSerial Dual-Port Passthrough Bridge
#include <SoftwareSerial.h>

// Instantiate virtual UART on digital pins: (RX, TX)
// Pin 10 connects to external device TX
// Pin 11 connects to external device RX
SoftwareSerial portTwo(10, 11);

void setup() {
  // Open hardware serial communication to PC Serial Monitor
  Serial.begin(9600);
  while (!Serial) { ; }

  // Open software serial communication to external peripheral
  portTwo.begin(9600);

  Serial.println("========================================");
  Serial.println("DUAL SERIAL PASSTHROUGH BRIDGE ACTIVE");
  Serial.println("Port 1: Hardware USB (Pins 0 & 1 @ 9600)");
  Serial.println("Port 2: SoftwareSerial (Pins 10 & 11 @ 9600)");
  Serial.println("========================================");
}

void loop() {
  // Forward bytes from SoftwareSerial (Port 2) to Hardware Serial (PC)
  if (portTwo.available()) {
    char inByte = portTwo.read();
    Serial.write(inByte); // write() sends raw binary byte without added newlines
  }

  // Forward bytes from Hardware Serial (PC) to SoftwareSerial (Port 2)
  if (Serial.available()) {
    char outByte = Serial.read();
    portTwo.write(outByte);
  }
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
1. `#include <SoftwareSerial.h>`: Imports the official Arduino virtual UART driver.
2. `SoftwareSerial portTwo(10, 11)`: Instantiates the port with Pin 10 as RX and Pin 11 as TX.
3. `Serial.write(inByte)`: Uses `write()` rather than `println()` so individual raw bytes pass across unchanged without appending carriage returns or newline characters.
